1
00:00:00,080 --> 00:00:03,875
The Post Office Riot of 1876

2
00:00:03,875 --> 00:00:08,205
On the morning of Friday
the 15th of December 1876,

3
00:00:08,285 --> 00:00:11,923
a small shop at 81
Market Street opened for

4
00:00:12,003 --> 00:00:16,500
business as Singapore's new
Chinese sub-post office.

5
00:00:16,500 --> 00:00:19,792
It had been set up by the
colonial government to

6
00:00:19,872 --> 00:00:23,093
carry Chinese workers'
letters and savings home

7
00:00:23,173 --> 00:00:26,475
to their families in
China, cheaply and safely.

8
00:00:26,475 --> 00:00:29,605
Soon afterwards a crowd
wrecked it, and by the

9
00:00:29,685 --> 00:00:32,815
end of the day the police
had fired on rioters

10
00:00:32,895 --> 00:00:36,175
at two police stations
and three men were dead.

11
00:00:36,175 --> 00:00:39,755
For decades, a Chinese
labourer in Singapore who

12
00:00:39,835 --> 00:00:43,800
wanted to send money home
had no post office to use.

13
00:00:43,800 --> 00:00:47,571
He gave his letter and a few
dollars to a trusted man from

14
00:00:47,651 --> 00:00:51,687
his own district, who carried
them back to China on the junks,

15
00:00:51,767 --> 00:00:55,750
or to one of the remittance
houses run by Chinese merchants.

16
00:00:55,750 --> 00:00:59,175
I wrote about that trade
in an earlier post.

17
00:00:59,175 --> 00:01:02,512
By the 1870s, the business
of collecting these

18
00:01:02,592 --> 00:01:05,632
letters and sending them on had come to be

19
00:01:05,712 --> 00:01:09,500
dominated by a few merchants,
most of them Teochew.

20
00:01:09,500 --> 00:01:13,063
From 1873, officials in
Singapore began to look at

21
00:01:13,143 --> 00:01:16,998
how the government could
offer the labourers a cheaper

22
00:01:17,078 --> 00:01:20,575
and safer way to send
their letters and savings.

23
00:01:20,575 --> 00:01:24,524
After talks with London, the
British minister in Peking,

24
00:01:24,604 --> 00:01:30,000
the consuls in China and the Hong Kong
authorities, they settled on a plan.

25
00:01:30,000 --> 00:01:35,028
A notice in the Government Gazette,
dated the 5th of December 1876,

26
00:01:35,108 --> 00:01:40,441
announced that a Chinese sub-post
office would open at 81 Market Street

27
00:01:40,521 --> 00:01:45,400
on the 15th of December, with
another at Beach Street in Penang.

28
00:01:45,400 --> 00:01:50,117
On the 11th of December the
Postmaster-General, Henry Trotter,

29
00:01:50,197 --> 00:01:54,375
issued a second notice in
Chinese to explain the plan.

30
00:01:54,375 --> 00:01:56,601
The small post offices, it said,

31
00:01:56,681 --> 00:02:00,500
were "for the benefit
especially of all you coolies."

32
00:02:00,500 --> 00:02:04,568
They would send letters to Swatow
and Amoy, and from there to

33
00:02:04,648 --> 00:02:09,000
any town or village in the interior,
and bring the replies back.

34
00:02:09,000 --> 00:02:12,271
They could also carry
money, at fixed charges

35
00:02:12,351 --> 00:02:15,850
set out in a table, with
no extra fees allowed.

36
00:02:15,850 --> 00:02:18,831
One line of the notice
said that when the system

37
00:02:18,911 --> 00:02:21,637
was in good working
order, all letters would

38
00:02:21,717 --> 00:02:24,650
have to go through the
government post office.

39
00:02:24,650 --> 00:02:27,021
The merchants who had run the letter

40
00:02:27,101 --> 00:02:29,825
business saw the plan as a threat to it.

41
00:02:29,825 --> 00:02:33,500
Within days, placards
went up around the town.

42
00:02:33,500 --> 00:02:37,416
One, dated the 13th of December,
called the men behind the

43
00:02:37,496 --> 00:02:41,618
post office "red rats" who had
schemed to make a fortune from

44
00:02:41,698 --> 00:02:45,625
the coolies' savings, and
cursed them and their families.

45
00:02:45,625 --> 00:02:48,905
It ended by offering a reward of 100 taels

46
00:02:48,985 --> 00:02:52,425
"for the heads of the
Post Office Farmers."

47
00:02:52,425 --> 00:02:57,121
A rumour spread among the labourers
that every man who wanted to send

48
00:02:57,201 --> 00:03:01,700
money home would first have to
pay 30 dollars to the post office.

49
00:03:01,700 --> 00:03:04,802
The government told the
leading Chinese merchants

50
00:03:04,882 --> 00:03:09,217
that the office was meant for the
convenience of the poorer classes,

51
00:03:09,297 --> 00:03:11,375
and the police took precautions.

52
00:03:11,375 --> 00:03:15,175
On the morning the office
opened, it was not enough.

53
00:03:15,175 --> 00:03:18,671
At about eight o'clock, soon
after the office opened,

54
00:03:18,751 --> 00:03:22,125
a crowd gathered in Market
Street and attacked it.

55
00:03:22,125 --> 00:03:25,330
Sticks and bottles were
thrown, and then the crowd

56
00:03:25,410 --> 00:03:28,813
broke into the shop, smashed
every piece of furniture

57
00:03:28,893 --> 00:03:32,375
and pulled down the royal
coat of arms over the door.

58
00:03:32,375 --> 00:03:36,078
The police under their
superintendent, R. W. Maxwell,

59
00:03:36,158 --> 00:03:39,362
arrived quickly, but the
crowd turned on them,

60
00:03:39,442 --> 00:03:42,725
and Maxwell was knocked
down and badly beaten.

61
00:03:42,725 --> 00:03:46,566
Part of the crowd then attacked
the police station at Telok

62
00:03:46,646 --> 00:03:50,500
Ayer and the one at Ellenborough
Market, across the river.

63
00:03:50,500 --> 00:03:53,375
At both, the police fired on them.

64
00:03:53,375 --> 00:03:57,025
Three men were killed
and several were wounded.

65
00:03:57,025 --> 00:04:00,094
Crowds stood around the
wrecked office for the rest

66
00:04:00,174 --> 00:04:03,200
of the day, while a police
guard held the street.

67
00:04:03,200 --> 00:04:05,950
The Straits Times was blunt.

68
00:04:05,950 --> 00:04:10,153
It called the riot "so senseless
as to be utterly inexcusable,"

69
00:04:10,233 --> 00:04:14,437
and it argued that the ordinary
labourers in the crowd had been

70
00:04:14,517 --> 00:04:18,800
"the mere dupes" of the men who
stood to lose the letter trade.

71
00:04:18,800 --> 00:04:23,336
Within a day, many of the rioters
were brought before the magistrate,

72
00:04:23,416 --> 00:04:25,825
Captain Walshe, at the Police Court.

73
00:04:25,825 --> 00:04:29,849
Of the first 25, seventeen
were sentenced to three months'

74
00:04:29,929 --> 00:04:33,458
rigorous imprisonment and
30 strokes of the rattan,

75
00:04:33,538 --> 00:04:38,350
though the colonial surgeon found
six of them unfit for the strokes.

76
00:04:38,350 --> 00:04:41,926
Three men who had shouted
to the others to fight were

77
00:04:42,006 --> 00:04:46,075
given six months, including a
week in solitary confinement.

78
00:04:46,075 --> 00:04:49,700
More men were tried
over the following days.

79
00:04:49,700 --> 00:04:52,500
The town did not settle at once.

80
00:04:52,500 --> 00:04:55,889
Over the weekend, business
stopped at Ellenborough

81
00:04:55,969 --> 00:04:58,675
Market and many provision shops closed.

82
00:04:58,675 --> 00:05:02,139
A notice posted near
the market told traders

83
00:05:02,219 --> 00:05:05,200
not to sell food to the "barbarians."

84
00:05:05,200 --> 00:05:08,988
The shopkeepers said they would
reopen only if the towkays,

85
00:05:09,068 --> 00:05:10,299
the merchant bosses,

86
00:05:10,379 --> 00:05:14,050
who were being held at the
police station were released.

87
00:05:14,050 --> 00:05:18,350
The government's answer was to
send twelve of them out to sea.

88
00:05:18,350 --> 00:05:21,553
Soldiers of the 80th Regiment
marched them from the

89
00:05:21,633 --> 00:05:25,286
Central Police Station along
Boat Quay to Johnston's Pier,

90
00:05:25,366 --> 00:05:29,341
between two columns of troops,
with crowds of Chinese watching,

91
00:05:29,421 --> 00:05:33,009
and a boat took them out to
the government steamer Pluto,

92
00:05:33,089 --> 00:05:36,050
which anchored three
miles out in the harbour.

93
00:05:36,050 --> 00:05:39,588
They were to stay there,
the Straits Times reported,

94
00:05:39,668 --> 00:05:44,400
"until all signs of rioting or
disobedience shall have disappeared."

95
00:05:44,400 --> 00:05:48,297
At the same time, about 600
marines and sailors from

96
00:05:48,377 --> 00:05:52,125
the navy's squadron were
paraded on Collyer Quay.

97
00:05:52,125 --> 00:05:54,648
The Colonial Secretary, John Douglas,

98
00:05:54,728 --> 00:05:58,950
called in other towkays and
warned them to open their shops.

99
00:05:58,950 --> 00:06:03,029
Years later, the merchant
W. H. Read recalled a scene

100
00:06:03,109 --> 00:06:06,875
that he believed belonged
to these disturbances.

101
00:06:06,875 --> 00:06:10,449
The north-east monsoon was
blowing, and from the shore

102
00:06:10,529 --> 00:06:14,250
he watched the steamer pitch
and roll in the heavy sea.

103
00:06:14,250 --> 00:06:17,103
After about two days,
he wrote, the men were

104
00:06:17,183 --> 00:06:20,250
brought ashore "pale,
prostrate and penitent."

105
00:06:20,250 --> 00:06:22,339
On Monday the 18th of December,

106
00:06:22,419 --> 00:06:26,537
the Postmaster-General reopened
the sub-post office himself,

107
00:06:26,617 --> 00:06:31,025
with the Colonial Secretary and
other officials there to watch.

108
00:06:31,025 --> 00:06:35,329
In 1877 the sub-post office
moved from Market Street into

109
00:06:35,409 --> 00:06:40,790
the General Post Office, under the
Postmaster-General's direct control,

110
00:06:40,870 --> 00:06:44,100
and the labourers slowly came to trust it.

111
00:06:44,100 --> 00:06:49,296
About 80,000 Chinese letters
went through it in 1880,

112
00:06:49,376 --> 00:06:55,050
and by 1889 the number had
risen to about 280,000 a year.

113
00:06:55,050 --> 00:06:58,575
The private letter
shops did not disappear.

114
00:06:58,575 --> 00:07:02,826
They were brought under the
Post Office's supervision,

115
00:07:02,906 --> 00:07:06,675
and in 1887 there were
49 of them in Singapore.

116
00:07:06,675 --> 00:07:09,633
The next year, the government set up a new

117
00:07:09,713 --> 00:07:12,750
office to deal with the Chinese community.

118
00:07:12,750 --> 00:07:16,676
William Pickering, an official
who spoke several Chinese

119
00:07:16,756 --> 00:07:20,467
languages and had translated
the Colonial Secretary's

120
00:07:20,547 --> 00:07:23,829
warning to the traders
during the disturbances,

121
00:07:23,909 --> 00:07:28,693
was appointed the first Protector
of Chinese on the 3rd of May 1877,

122
00:07:28,773 --> 00:07:32,350
with an office in a shophouse
on North Canal Road.

123
00:07:32,350 --> 00:07:35,613
The Chinese Protectorate
dealt with the arrival

124
00:07:35,693 --> 00:07:38,885
of labourers from China,
the secret societies,

125
00:07:38,965 --> 00:07:42,450
and the rescue of women
forced into prostitution.

126
00:07:42,450 --> 00:07:47,275
Who was really behind the riot
has been argued over ever since.

127
00:07:47,275 --> 00:07:50,417
Officials and the
newspapers of the day blamed

128
00:07:50,497 --> 00:07:54,000
the secret societies, and
the Ghee Hin among them.

129
00:07:54,000 --> 00:07:58,010
Some historians have argued since
that it was the owners of the

130
00:07:58,090 --> 00:08:01,775
remittance businesses themselves
who stirred up the crowd,

131
00:08:01,855 --> 00:08:06,400
to defend their trade against a
government that wanted to regulate it.

132
00:08:06,400 --> 00:08:10,617
Writing years later, the Straits
Chinese doctor Lim Boon Keng

133
00:08:10,697 --> 00:08:14,773
used the riot as an example of
how much trouble between the

134
00:08:14,853 --> 00:08:19,150
Chinese and the Europeans "has
arisen from misunderstanding."

135
00:08:19,150 --> 00:08:23,900
Singapore's migrant workers still
send part of their pay home.

136
00:08:23,900 --> 00:08:27,356
The money now goes by bank
transfer, by mobile app,

137
00:08:27,436 --> 00:08:30,408
or through the money
changers and remittance

138
00:08:30,488 --> 00:08:33,400
counters in neighbourhoods like Chinatown.

139
00:08:33,400 --> 00:08:37,184
Since 2020, businesses that
send money abroad have been

140
00:08:37,264 --> 00:08:41,539
licensed and supervised by the
Monetary Authority of Singapore

141
00:08:41,619 --> 00:08:45,121
under the Payment Services
Act, so the question the

142
00:08:45,201 --> 00:08:49,547
government was trying to answer
in 1876, of how to make sending

143
00:08:49,627 --> 00:08:53,350
money home cheap and safe,
is still part of its work.

144
00:08:53,350 --> 00:08:58,546
Where it fits in the bigger story: The
Post Office riot is usually remembered,

145
00:08:58,626 --> 00:09:01,928
if at all, as one more
secret-society disturbance,

146
00:09:02,008 --> 00:09:05,039
but it was really a fight
over who would carry

147
00:09:05,119 --> 00:09:08,975
the savings of Singapore's
poorest workers home to China.

148
00:09:08,975 --> 00:09:12,549
It began with a public notice
and a rumour about what

149
00:09:12,629 --> 00:09:16,340
that notice meant, and it
ended with a post office that

150
00:09:16,420 --> 00:09:20,350
the labourers came to use in
their hundreds of thousands.

151
00:09:20,350 --> 00:09:23,953
It is a reminder that a scheme
meant to help people can

152
00:09:24,033 --> 00:09:28,050
fail at first if the people it
is meant for do not trust it.
